A||||4||BENGAL4||Wet Sensors?||||1||
Z||000000||BENGAL4||05-25-2000||03:36 PM||||A freind of mine has a 2.8 in a 86SE. He had the engine steam cleaned and now it hesitates during acceleration. I told him the sensors must have gotten wet. Can he dry the sensors or should he just wait for them to air dry?. HELP. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierospeeder||05-25-2000||04:14 PM||||If he has bad plug wires, and they got wet, the car will hesitate until it drys out.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||05-25-2000||04:54 PM||||Check for moisture inside the distributor cap also.||||reg||3
Z||000003||DRH||05-25-2000||05:02 PM||||Oops, didn't address the original question. A steam cleaner can force moisture into the "weather proof" connectors. You may have to unplug them to get it back out again. A hair dryer works pretty good for speeding up the evaporation process. If it belongs to your wife, just don't let her catch you with it in the garage though.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'd still check the dist. cap, wires and coil connections first though...<P>||||reg||11
Z||000004||theogre||05-26-2000||12:02 PM||||if you sprayed the TPS or MAP you may have to replace them. Once the TPS get water in it's not coming out soon. cleaning chemicals can scrag the MAP. It depends exactly what make made the MAP. Wells, ACDelco, and most otheres have vent hole(s) on top. BorgWarner put the vent on the bottom where it's less likely to let junk get to the guts of it. (AT least I think it's on bottom... it's not on top.)<P>Check and dry everything else out first. If it still acts up then one or both the above may have been messed up.<P>also check that you didn't blow off or damage any vacuum lines.||||reg||1
